    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-1",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "1 · Two jobs, two minds",
      "sentence": "Divergent work multiplies kinds; convergent work finds the fit. Schedule them apart.",
      "image_brief": "Many concept paths before one choice.",
      "quote": "Craft notes that one creative act may hold both divergent and convergent thinking.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Craft 2000, 30)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/self-creativity-attention/#ref-craft-2000"
      },
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Selig presents Mike's Brainstorm An exceptional wild animal comedy. LCCN2015645268.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-SCA.masterclass-1",
      "notes": "Bridge to U1/U2. Chen/Guilford framing stays available; keep classroom language simple."
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-2",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "2 · Open-monitoring vs focused attention",
      "sentence": "OM notices what arises without fixing; FA returns to one object. They are not the same practice.",
      "image_brief": "Upright still attention — a posture of noticing or of return.",
      "quote": "OM meditation induces a control state that promotes divergent thinking.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Colzato, Ozturk, and Hommel 2012, 1)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/self-creativity-attention/#ref-colzato-2012"
      },
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Caspar David Friedrich - Wanderer above the sea of fog.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-SCA.masterclass-2",
      "notes": "Definitions from the study Method. Classroom dose later ≠ 35 min."
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-3",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "3 · Opening feeds ideation",
      "sentence": "After open-monitoring, people did better on a divergent task in the lab study.",
      "image_brief": "Many sticky notes — fluency before judgement.",
      "quote": "First, OM meditation induces a control state that promotes divergent thinking.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Colzato, Ozturk, and Hommel 2012, 1)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/self-creativity-attention/#ref-colzato-2012"
      },
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Stickies to brainstorm Edit.2014..jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-SCA.masterclass-3",
      "notes": "Honest: one small laboratory study. Not a talent claim."
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-4",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "4 · Focus is not magic selection",
      "sentence": "Why: the study predicted focused-attention would help convergent thinking — it did not hold up. What then: use FA to return to one object when you already have options; do not expect it to invent the right pick for you.",
      "image_brief": "A careful stack that can still fall — focus without guarantee.",
      "quote": "FA meditation did not sustain convergent thinking as predicted.",
      "quote_origin": "tao_invented",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Tao of Creativity — after Colzato et al. 2012)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/self-creativity-attention/#ref-colzato-2012"
      },
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Jean Siméon Chardin, The House of Cards, probably 1737, NGA 97.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-SCA.masterclass-4",
      "notes": "Why/what-then: students must not leave thinking FA = automatic good picks. Convergent work still needs criteria, comparison, and craft — attention style is only one tool."
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-5",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "5 · Incubation needs partial attention",
      "sentence": "Walk, shower, craft — leave a problem half-held so new links can surface.",
      "image_brief": "Quiet making that leaves room for thought to arrive.",
      "quote": "The less you try to direct the process the more creative the conclusions are likely to be.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Csikszentmihalyi 1996)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/self-creativity-attention/#ref-csikszentmihalyi-1996"
      },
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Johannes Vermeer - A Lady Writing - Google Art Project.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-SCA.masterclass-5",
      "notes": "Personal creativity / incubation chapter language — keep classroom paraphrase short."
    },
    {
